$articles=[
['A more thoughtful way to find a home in Colombo','thoughtful-way-to-find-a-home-colombo','Buying well','A clear-eyed guide to neighbourhoods, daily rhythms and the small details that make a city home feel right.','Colombo is not one place but a collection of distinct rhythms. Begin with the ordinary: your commute, the morning light, a walkable coffee, a school run or the park you return to. The right home supports your daily life before it impresses on a viewing.\n\nCinnamon Gardens rewards those who value trees, institutions and established streets. Colombo 03 balances the city’s energy with the coast. Rajagiriya and Kotte offer more space and a growing network of cafés, schools and offices.\n\nLook closely at orientation, cross ventilation, water pressure, backup power and the quality of shared management. Ask for the deed, survey plan and condominium documentation early. A considered decision is usually the result of good questions, not a rushed offer.','photo-1600607687920-4e2a09cf159d'],
['The south coast, beyond the weekend','south-coast-beyond-weekend','Places','From Galle Fort to Hiriketiya, a closer look at the coast as a place to live, not simply escape to.','A weekend on the southern coast is easy to fall for. Living here asks for a different kind of attention. The rhythm shifts with the monsoon, the village calendar and the road.\n\nGalle offers year-round community, schools and services alongside its extraordinary heritage. Ahangama and Weligama have become creative, well-connected bases. Further east, smaller bays offer a quieter life and a closer relationship with the seasons.\n\nBefore choosing a coastal address, understand the shoreline setback, flood history, water source and access in every season. Spend time in the neighbourhood beyond the holiday months. The best coastal homes belong to their place.','photo-1600607687644-c7171b42498f'],
['What makes a home feel cooler in the tropics?','homes-that-breathe-tropical-design','Architecture','Shade, airflow and orientation are timeless comforts in Sri Lanka’s warm climate.','The most comfortable tropical homes are often the ones that work with their climate. A deep eave can protect a room from afternoon sun; a shaded veranda makes the transition between indoors and out feel natural.\n\nCross ventilation depends on openings placed with care, not simply more glass. Courtyards can draw daylight into the centre of a plan while planting softens heat and glare. Materials matter too: they should be durable, repairable and suited to humidity.\n\nWhen viewing a home, notice the air at midday. Check where the sun falls, how the rooms breathe and whether the outdoor shade is usable. Comfort is a design decision you experience every day.','photo-1600566753086-00f18fb6b3ea'],
['A guide to buying a second home in Sri Lanka','buying-a-second-home-sri-lanka','Buying well','The practical questions to answer before you commit to a weekend home or island retreat.','A second home should make your life richer without becoming a source of constant administration. Start with how often you will use it and who will care for it when you are away.\n\nUnderstand the ownership structure and have an independent Sri Lankan lawyer review title, approvals and boundaries. For coastal and hill-country land, check access rights, drainage and any relevant development restrictions.\n\nBuild a realistic annual budget for caretaking, garden maintenance, insurance, utilities and travel. Choose a place you enjoy in its quieter moments as much as its most beautiful ones. A trusted local network is as valuable as the house itself.','photo-1600210492486-724fe5c67fb0'],
['Kandy living: a city held by the hills','kandy-living-city-held-by-hills','Places','A slower pace, a rich cultural life and the everyday pleasures of Sri Lanka’s hill capital.','Kandy’s geography makes it feel intimate. Hills fold around the lake and the old city, while Peradeniya and Katugastota each bring a different pace and character.\n\nThe cooler evenings change how a home is used. Verandas and gardens are living rooms; views become part of the day. For families, proximity to schools and reliable access to the city centre can shape a comfortable routine.\n\nConsider the slope, retaining walls and drainage as carefully as the outlook. A well-sited home can feel quietly connected to its setting while remaining practical through the rain and the busy season.','photo-1600607687939-ce8a6c25118c'],
['The details that make a well-designed apartment','details-of-a-well-designed-apartment','Design notes','Beyond the brochure: the practical details that shape apartment living over time.','A beautiful lobby is a first impression. Everyday apartment life is made by quieter details: lift reliability, sound between rooms, natural light, storage and the way the building is managed.\n\nAsk to see the condominium plan, house rules, maintenance history and sinking fund. Visit at different times if you can. Notice how the shared spaces feel and whether the building is cared for.\n\nInside the home, look at usable wall space, kitchen ventilation, laundry placement and the balcony’s real dimensions. A well-designed apartment does not ask you to compromise on ordinary comfort.','photo-1600573472591-ee6b68d14c68'],
];
